= Knowledge representation — Technical =

== Five roles ==
Davis, Shrobe and Szolovits characterize KR as:

# a surrogate for the world;
# a set of ontological commitments;
# a fragmentary theory of intelligent reasoning;
# a medium for efficient computation;
# a medium of human expression.

These roles explain why representation-language design cannot be evaluated only by expressivity.

== Declarative semantics ==
A KR language L usually defines:

<pre>
syntax(L)
semantics(L)
inference(L)
</pre>

Semantics maps expressions to interpretations.

Inference procedures derive consequences.

Their relationship is evaluated through soundness and completeness.

== Expressivity / complexity ==
More expressive languages can represent more distinctions but make reasoning harder.

Typical complexity questions include:
* satisfiability;
* entailment;
* query answering;
* classification;
* model checking.

Description logics occupy a designed region between weak frame systems and unrestricted first-order logic.

== Non-monotonicity ==
Default reasoning may use forms such as:

<pre>
Bird(x) : normally Fly(x)
</pre>

where exceptions defeat conclusions.

Approaches include:
* default logic;
* circumscription;
* answer-set programming;
* defeasible logic.

These differ fundamentally from monotonic OWL entailment.

== Hybrid representations ==
Modern KR systems may combine:
* symbolic triples;
* rules;
* embeddings;
* vector retrieval;
* probabilistic models;
* language models.

The numerical layer may suggest candidates while the symbolic layer enforces explicit semantics.

A hybrid system should not present learned similarity as if it were deductive entailment.

